 Also called: Periodic 3D Value Noise
 ## Overview
-Tiled Value Noise is a procedural noise generation method that produces seamless, periodic 3D scalar fields by wrapping the lattice indices of a standard Value Noise implementation. It solves the problem of visible discontinuities and directional stripe artifacts that occur when standard value noise is tiled or repeated, which are typically caused by fixed basis functions (like sine/cosine) or non-periodic interpolation boundaries [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard Value Noise, which assumes an infinite domain, Tiled Value Noise constrains the signal to a specific frequency-based period, ensuring that the noise pattern matches perfectly at the boundaries of the tile [S1, unofficial]. It serves as the foundational noise primitive for higher-level techniques such as Fractal Value Noise with Domain Warping, where seamless tiling is required to avoid visible seams in the warped domain [8323-fractal-value-noise-with-domain-warping.md].
